𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗯𝗼𝗼𝗸 "𝗦𝗽𝗲𝗲𝗱 𝗼𝗳 𝗧𝗿𝘂𝘀𝘁" by Steven Covey was release 20 years ago. The basic concept still resonates with me today. Trust = character (integrity + intent) and competence (capabilities + results). Speed drives growth and competitive advantage. But how has that book held up overtime? 

The core definition, at least on a micro level, has help up extremely well. The marketing-to-the-masses Covey style make the book very accessible. The repetitive nature helps memorize the core idea. However, the book was written before Enron, SOX, and COSO ERM. The book does a great job framing individual trust but lacks in providing a framework to build organizational trust.  

That's where COSO ERM framework comes into play, providing framework for the company as a whole. The COSO ERM framework has five components that can be easily broken down into 𝘁𝘄𝗼 Covey-like marketing-to-the-masses concepts: 𝗘𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝗽𝗿𝗶𝘀𝗲 𝗧𝗿𝘂𝘀𝘁 = 𝗦𝘁𝗿𝗮𝘁𝗲𝗴𝘆 + 𝗘𝘅𝗲𝗰𝘂𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻. Strategy is first two ERM components (Governance/culture + Strategy/objective setting) and Execution is the last three (Performance + Review/revision + Reporting). Said another way,  

• Who are we as a company?
• What do we want to accomplish?
• How are we going to get there?
• How did we do in getting there?
• What do we need to improve?
• Rinse and repeat.

Paired with Covey's framework for individual trust, we get the holistic framework for building a fast, nimble, and scalable company structure.  

Putting it all together:  

• 𝗧𝗼𝘁𝗮𝗹 𝗧𝗿𝘂𝘀𝘁 = Individual Trust + Enterprise Trust
• 𝗜𝗻𝗱𝗶𝘃𝗶𝗱𝘂𝗮𝗹 𝗧𝗿𝘂𝘀𝘁 = Character (integrity + intent) + Competence (capabilities + results).
• 𝗘𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝗽𝗿𝗶𝘀𝗲 𝗧𝗿𝘂𝘀𝘁 = Strategy (governance + goal-setting) + Execution (risk assessment + monitoring + reporting)

𝗕𝗼𝘁𝘁𝗼𝗺 𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗲: Trust scales when credible people operate inside credible systems. 

